{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LsoyYL2kVdq2WTqsnLYkdP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"check_complexity.py — the stdlib cognitive-complexity + SLOC census.\n3\t\n4\tMeasures SonarSource Cognitive Complexity (CognitiveComplexity.pdf v1.7) and\n5\tSLOC per function, plus a per-module public-symbol count (reported, never\n6\tgated — the Ousterhout depth proxy). Stdlib only: a shipped check must not make\n7\tevery adopter install a linter, which is why this exists beside — and not on\n8\ttop of — the ruff C901 ratchet. Because the counting rules live in this\n9\tdocstring rather than in a third-party tool, the baseline is a property of the\n10\tcode, not of a tool version: a linter upgrade cannot silently invalidate it.\n11\t\n12\tREPORT-ONLY as shipped: no `[step:]` is wired into `docs/stack.ini`, so nothing\n13\truns this as a gate. The enforce capability exists (`--mode enforce`, exercised\n14\tby the tests) but arming it is an opt-in act — a `[step:complexity]` step, of\n15\tthe shape below:\n16\t\n17\t    [step:complexity]\n18\t    command = {py} project-trajectory/scripts/check_complexity.py --root .\n19\t\n20\tCENSUS UNIT. One row per module-level function and per method. A nested `def`\n21\tor `lambda` is scored INTO its enclosing function (Sonar charges the nesting\n22\tincrement for it, never a base increment) and never gets its own row: the\n23\treward for decomposition is a SIBLING function or a new module, not an inner\n24\tone, and a second row for the inner def would double-count it in the census.\n25\t\n26\tBASELINE. `docs/complexity-baseline`, TSV, LF-only, sorted by path then\n27\tfunction, one row per over-threshold function: path, function, cognitive, sloc,\n28\treason. Its own header states that it is a DEBT STATEMENT, not an approval. TSV\n29\tfor one reason — the minimum merge-conflict surface when two concurrent\n30\tsessions re-stamp; it matches the extension-less house data files under `docs/`\n31\t(`docs/coverage-floors`). There is NO inline suppression pragma and there must\n32\tnever be one; the escape hatch is this one reviewed central file, because a\n33\tscattered opt-out self-replicates as new code copies it.\n34\t\n35\tImplements: SR-183, LLR-206\n36\t\"\"\"\n37\t\n38\timport argparse\n39\timport ast\n40\timport sys\n41\tfrom pathlib import Path\n42\t\n43\tfrom kitlib.config import utf8_console\n44\t\n45\tDEFAULT_THRESHOLD = 15\n46\tBASELINE = \"docs/complexity-baseline\"\n47\tDEFAULT_INCLUDE = (\"project-trajectory/scripts/**/*.py\",)\n48\tCOLUMNS = (\"path\", \"function\", \"cognitive\", \"sloc\", \"reason\")\n49\tHEADER = \"# \" + \"\\t\".join(COLUMNS)\n50\tDEBT_NOTE = (\n51\t    \"# docs/complexity-baseline — one row per function over the cognitive-\"\n52\t    \"complexity threshold when stamped.\\n\"\n53\t    \"# A row is a DEBT STATEMENT, NOT AN APPROVAL: the reason column records \"\n54\t    \"why it is carried, and the\\n\"\n55\t    \"# baseline only ever tightens — re-stamp DOWNWARD or delete a row, never \"\n56\t    \"up to clear a finding.\\n\"\n57\t)\n58\tPYCACHE = \"__pycache__\"\n59\tFIXES = (\n60\t    \"  named escapes: decompose OUTWARD (a sibling function or a new module, \"\n61\t    \"never a nested def); express the branches as a data table \"\n62\t    \"(check.py::steps is 670 lines at cognitive 14); define the error out of \"\n63\t    \"existence so the branch disappears.\"\n64\t)\n65\t\n66\t_LOOPS = (ast.For, ast.AsyncFor, ast.While)\n67\t_COMPS = (ast.ListComp, ast.SetComp, ast.DictComp, ast.GeneratorExp)\n68\t_DEFS = (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef)\n69\t\n70\t\n71\tdef _kids(node, nesting, ctx):\n72\t    return sum(_visit(c, nesting, ctx) for c in ast.iter_child_nodes(node))\n73\t\n74\t\n75\tdef _seq(stmts, nesting, ctx):\n76\t    return sum(_visit(s, nesting, ctx) for s in stmts)\n77\t\n78\t\n79\tdef _h_if(node, nesting, ctx):\n80\t    return (\n81\t        1\n82\t        + nesting\n83\t        + _visit(node.test, nesting, ctx)\n84\t        + _seq(node.body, nesting + 1, ctx)\n85\t        + _h_orelse(node, nesting, ctx)\n86\t    )\n87\t\n88\t\n89\tdef _h_orelse(node, nesting, ctx):\n90\t    \"\"\"TRAP 1. Python parses `elif` as an `If` nested in `orelse`, so a naive\n91\t    recursion both double-counts and over-nests every ladder in the tree. An\n92\t    `elif` is HYBRID: +1 flat, NO nesting increment, body one level deeper. A\n93\t    written-out `else:` is +1 flat with its body one deeper. The discriminator\n94\t    is the column — a real `elif` starts at the parent `if`'s column.\"\"\"\n95\t    tail = node.orelse\n96\t    if not tail:\n97\t        return 0\n98\t    head = tail[0]\n99\t    chained = len(tail) == 1 and isinstance(head, ast.If)\n100\t    if chained and head.col_offset == node.col_offset:\n101\t        return (\n102\t            1\n103\t            + _visit(head.test, nesting, ctx)\n104\t            + _seq(head.body, nesting + 1, ctx)\n105\t            + _h_orelse(head, nesting, ctx)\n106\t        )\n107\t    return _h_else(tail, nesting, ctx)\n108\t\n109\t\n110\tdef _h_else(stmts, nesting, ctx):\n111\t    \"\"\"`else` — on an `if`, and equally on Python's loop-`else`, which is a\n112\t    break in linear flow and a famously misread one: +1 flat, body one deeper.\n113\t    The spec has no loop-`else` (no Java analogue); the reference Python\n114\t    implementation charges it, and so does this.\"\"\"\n115\t    return (1 + _seq(stmts, nesting + 1, ctx)) if stmts else 0\n116\t\n117\t\n118\tdef _h_loop(node, nesting, ctx):\n119\t    head = node.test if isinstance(node, ast.While) else node.iter\n120\t    return (\n121\t        1\n122\t        + nesting\n123\t        + _visit(head, nesting, ctx)\n124\t        + _seq(node.body, nesting + 1, ctx)\n125\t        + _h_else(node.orelse, nesting, ctx)\n126\t    )\n127\t\n128\t\n129\tdef _h_ifexp(node, nesting, ctx):\n130\t    return (\n131\t        1\n132\t        + nesting\n133\t        + _visit(node.test, nesting, ctx)\n134\t        + _visit(node.body, nesting + 1, ctx)\n135\t        + _visit(node.orelse, nesting + 1, ctx)\n136\t    )\n137\t\n138\t\n139\tdef _h_except(node, nesting, ctx):\n140\t    \"\"\"A catch is +1 however many types it names; `try`, `else` and `finally`\n141\t    are ignored altogether (spec, \"Catches\") — they fall through to `_kids`.\"\"\"\n142\t    return 1 + nesting + _seq(node.body, nesting + 1, ctx)\n143\t\n144\t\n145\tdef _h_match(node, nesting, ctx):\n146\t    \"\"\"A `switch` and ALL its cases combined is ONE structural increment (spec,\n147\t    \"Switches\"): it is read at a glance, unlike an if/elif ladder that may\n148\t    compare any number of variables against anything. A `case ... if guard`\n149\t    adds +1 flat — the guard is the extra comparison a switch cannot express.\"\"\"\n150\t    total = 1 + nesting + _visit(node.subject, nesting, ctx)\n151\t    for case in node.cases:\n152\t        if case.guard is not None:\n153\t            total += 1 + _visit(case.guard, nesting, ctx)\n154\t        total += _seq(case.body, nesting + 1, ctx)\n155\t    return total\n156\t\n157\t\n158\tdef _h_boolop(node, nesting, ctx):\n159\t    \"\"\"TRAP 2. +1 per RUN of like operators, not per operator: `a and b and c`\n160\t    is +1, `a and b or c` is +2. Python's parser already groups a run into one\n161\t    BoolOp with n values, so counting BoolOp NODES is counting runs.\"\"\"\n162\t    return 1 + _kids(node, nesting, ctx)\n163\t\n164\t\n165\tdef _h_lambda(node, nesting, ctx):\n166\t    return _kids(node, nesting + 1, ctx)\n167\t\n168\t\n169\tdef _h_comp(node, nesting, ctx):\n170\t    \"\"\"A comprehension is its own scope: nesting increment, no base increment\n171\t    (like a lambda). Each `if` clause is a real condition: +1 + inner nesting.\"\"\"\n172\t    inner = nesting + 1\n173\t    clauses = sum(1 for gen in node.generators for _ in gen.ifs)\n174\t    return _kids(node, inner, ctx) + clauses * (1 + inner)\n175\t\n176\t\n177\tdef _h_def(node, nesting, ctx):\n178\t    \"\"\"A nested def takes the nesting increment and no base increment — unless\n179\t    Appendix A's Python-decorator exception exempts it.\"\"\"\n180\t    return _kids(node, nesting + (0 if id(node) in ctx[1] else 1), ctx)\n181\t\n182\t\n183\tdef _h_call(node, nesting, ctx):\n184\t    \"\"\"Direct recursion is +1 per recursive call site: a bare `name(...)` or a\n185\t    `self`/`cls`-qualified `self.name(...)`. Anything else qualified is a\n186\t    DELEGATION, not recursion — `RoutingState.cool` calling `agent_route.cool`\n187\t    is the case that a bare attribute-name match gets wrong. Indirect recursion\n188\t    (the spec's \"recursion cycle\") needs a whole-program call graph and is out\n189\t    of scope.\"\"\"\n190\t    func = node.func\n191\t    owner = getattr(func, \"value\", None)\n192\t    same = getattr(owner, \"id\", None) in (\"self\", \"cls\")\n193\t    hit = (\n194\t        func.id\n195\t        if isinstance(func, ast.Name)\n196\t        else (getattr(func, \"attr\", None) if same else None)\n197\t    )\n198\t    return (1 if hit == ctx[0] else 0) + _kids(node, nesting, ctx)\n199\t\n200\t\n201\t_HANDLERS = {\n202\t    ast.If: _h_if,\n203\t    ast.IfExp: _h_ifexp,\n204\t    ast.ExceptHandler: _h_except,\n205\t    ast.BoolOp: _h_boolop,\n206\t    ast.Lambda: _h_lambda,\n207\t    ast.Call: _h_call,\n208\t}\n209\t_HANDLERS.update({t: _h_loop for t in _LOOPS})\n210\t_HANDLERS.update({t: _h_comp for t in _COMPS})\n211\t_HANDLERS.update({t: _h_def for t in _DEFS})\n212\tif hasattr(ast, \"Match\"):\n213\t    _HANDLERS[ast.Match] = _h_match\n214\t\n215\t\n216\tdef _visit(node, nesting, ctx):\n217\t    if node is None:\n218\t        return 0\n219\t    handler = _HANDLERS.get(type(node))\n220\t    return handler(node, nesting, ctx) if handler else _kids(node, nesting, ctx)\n221\t\n222\t\n223\tdef _is_docstring(stmt):\n224\t    return (\n225\t        isinstance(stmt, ast.Expr)\n226\t        and isinstance(stmt.value, ast.Constant)\n227\t        and isinstance(stmt.value.value, str)\n228\t    )\n229\t\n230\t\n231\tdef _wrapped_def(node):\n232\t    \"\"\"The inner `def` of Appendix A's Python-decorator shape — a body of\n233\t    nothing but an optional docstring, one nested def, and a `return` OF THAT\n234\t    DEF. Else None. The paper says \"only a nested function and a return\n235\t    statement\"; requiring the return to name the def is the narrow reading it\n236\t    asks for, and it is what stops a plain `def helper(): ...; return\n237\t    (helper(a), helper(b))` from claiming a decorator's exemption.\"\"\"\n238\t    body = [s for s in node.body if not _is_docstring(s)]\n239\t    if (\n240\t        len(body) != 2\n241\t        or not isinstance(body[0], _DEFS)\n242\t        or not isinstance(body[1], ast.Return)\n243\t    ):\n244\t        return None\n245\t    return body[0] if getattr(body[1].value, \"id\", None) == body[0].name else None\n246\t\n247\t\n248\tdef _exempt(fn):\n249\t    out, node = set(), _wrapped_def(fn)\n250\t    while node is not None:\n251\t        out.add(id(node))\n252\t        node = _wrapped_def(node)\n253\t    return out\n254\t\n255\t\n256\tdef cognitive(fn):\n257\t    \"\"\"The Sonar cognitive complexity of `fn`, nested defs and lambdas\n258\t    included.\"\"\"\n259\t    return _seq(fn.body, 0, (fn.name, _exempt(fn)))\n260\t\n261\t\n262\tdef _doc_lines(tree):\n263\t    out = set()\n264\t    for node in ast.walk(tree):\n265\t        if isinstance(node, (ast.Module, ast.ClassDef) + _DEFS) and node.body:\n266\t            head = node.body[0]\n267\t            if _is_docstring(head):\n268\t                out.update(range(head.lineno, head.end_lineno + 1))\n269\t    return out\n270\t\n271\t\n272\tdef sloc(node, lines, doc_lines):\n273\t    \"\"\"Non-blank, non-comment, non-docstring physical lines of `node`. Half the\n274\t    kit-scripts tree is prose by house style, so a raw line count would be\n275\t    measuring documentation.\"\"\"\n276\t    live = [i for i in range(node.lineno, node.end_lineno + 1) if i not in doc_lines]\n277\t    body = [lines[i - 1].strip() for i in live]\n278\t    return sum(1 for text in body if text and not text.startswith(\"#\"))\n279\t\n280\t\n281\tdef _collect(node, prefix, out):\n282\t    \"\"\"Every module-level function and method, qualified `Class.method`.\"\"\"\n283\t    for field in (\"body\", \"orelse\", \"finalbody\", \"handlers\"):\n284\t        for child in getattr(node, field, None) or []:\n285\t            if isinstance(child, ast.ClassDef):\n286\t                _collect(child, prefix + child.name + \".\", out)\n287\t            elif isinstance(child, _DEFS):\n288\t                out.append((prefix + child.name, child))\n289\t            elif isinstance(child, (ast.If, ast.Try, ast.With)):\n290\t                _collect(child, prefix, out)\n291\t\n292\t\n293\tdef _public(tree):\n294\t    names = []\n295\t    for node in tree.body:\n296\t        if isinstance(node, (ast.ClassDef,) + _DEFS):\n297\t            names.append(node.name)\n298\t        elif isinstance(node, ast.Assign):\n299\t            names += [t.id for t in node.targets if isinstance(t, ast.Name)]\n300\t    return [n for n in names if not n.startswith(\"_\")]\n301\t\n302\t\n303\tdef _paths(root, includes):\n304\t    seen = set()\n305\t    for pattern in includes:\n306\t        for path in sorted(root.glob(pattern)):\n307\t            rel = path.relative_to(root).as_posix()\n308\t            if PYCACHE not in path.parts and path.is_file() and rel not in seen:\n309\t                seen.add(rel)\n310\t                yield rel, path\n311\t\n312\t\n313\tdef census(root, includes):\n314\t    \"\"\"`(rows, modules)` — every function as `(path, name, cognitive, sloc)`\n315\t    and every module as `(path, public_symbols, lines)`. Paths are POSIX and\n316\t    relative to `root`, so a baseline is byte-identical on Windows and POSIX.\"\"\"\n317\t    rows, modules = [], []\n318\t    for rel, path in _paths(root, includes):\n319\t        text = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n320\t        tree = ast.parse(text)\n321\t        lines, docs = text.splitlines(), _doc_lines(tree)\n322\t        found = []\n323\t        _collect(tree, \"\", found)\n324\t        rows += [(rel, n, cognitive(f), sloc(f, lines, docs)) for n, f in found]\n325\t        modules.append((rel, len(_public(tree)), len(lines)))\n326\t    return sorted(rows), sorted(modules)\n327\t\n328\t\n329\tdef read_baseline(path):\n330\t    \"\"\"`{(path, function): (cognitive, sloc, reason)}`; empty when unstamped.\"\"\"\n331\t    out = {}\n332\t    if not path.exists():\n333\t        return out\n334\t    for line in path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines():\n335\t        if line.startswith(\"#\"):\n336\t            continue\n337\t        cells = line.split(\"\\t\")\n338\t        if len(cells) < 4:\n339\t            continue\n340\t        reason = cells[4] if len(cells) > 4 else \"\"\n341\t        out[(cells[0], cells[1])] = (int(cells[2]), int(cells[3]), reason)\n342\t    return out\n343\t\n344\t\n345\tdef write_baseline(path, rows, old):\n346\t    text = [DEBT_NOTE + HEADER]\n347\t    for rel, name, cog, lines in rows:\n348\t        reason = old.get((rel, name), (\"\", \"\", \"\"))[2]\n349\t        text.append(\"\\t\".join((rel, name, str(cog), str(lines), reason)))\n350\t    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n351\t    with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\") as handle:\n352\t        handle.write(\"\\n\".join(text) + \"\\n\")\n353\t\n354\t\n355\tdef compare(over, old):\n356\t    \"\"\"`(grew, improved)` as `[(path, function, was, now)]` — rows that rose\n357\t    above the baseline (or are new to it), and rows that fell below it (or\n358\t    vanished, reported as `now=None`).\"\"\"\n359\t    now = {(rel, name): cog for rel, name, cog, _ in over}\n360\t    grew, improved = [], []\n361\t    for key, score in sorted(now.items()):\n362\t        was = old[key][0] if key in old else None\n363\t        if was is None or score > was:\n364\t            grew.append(key + (was, score))\n365\t    for key, entry in sorted(old.items()):\n366\t        if now.get(key, -1) < entry[0]:\n367\t            improved.append(key + (entry[0], now.get(key)))\n368\t    return grew, improved\n369\t\n370\t\n371\tdef _fail(grew, improved):\n372\t    for rel, name, was, score in grew:\n373\t        print(\n374\t            \"check_complexity: FAIL - {}::{} cognitive {} -> {}. SIMPLIFY; a \"\n375\t            \"bump is a reviewed baseline edit whose reason lands in the log, \"\n376\t            \"never a drive-by.\\n{}\".format(rel, name, was, score, FIXES),\n377\t            file=sys.stderr,\n378\t        )\n379\t    for rel, name, was, score in improved:\n380\t        print(\n381\t            \"check_complexity: FAIL - {}::{} cognitive {} -> {}. RE-STAMP \"\n382\t            \"DOWNWARD (or delete the row) in this same commit, so the ratchet \"\n383\t            \"only ever tightens; `--restamp` writes it.\".format(rel, name, was, score),\n384\t            file=sys.stderr,\n385\t        )\n386\t\n387\t\n388\tdef _report(rows, modules, threshold):\n389\t    print(\"# \" + \"\\t\".join(COLUMNS[:4]))\n390\t    for row in rows:\n391\t        print(\"\\t\".join(str(cell) for cell in row))\n392\t    for cut in sorted({threshold, 25, 50}):\n393\t        print(\"# over {}: {}\".format(cut, sum(1 for r in rows if r[2] > cut)))\n394\t    for rel, public, lines in modules:\n395\t        print(\"# module\\t{}\\t{}\\t{}\".format(rel, public, lines))\n396\t\n397\t\n398\tdef main(argv=None):\n399\t    utf8_console()\n400\t    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n401\t        description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter\n402\t    )\n403\t    ap.add_argument(\"--root\", default=\".\", help=\"repo root (default: cwd)\")\n404\t    ap.add_argument(\"--mode\", choices=(\"report\", \"warn\", \"enforce\"), default=\"warn\")\n405\t    ap.add_argument(\"--report\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"alias for --mode report\")\n406\t    ap.add_argument(\"--restamp\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"rewrite the baseline\")\n407\t    ap.add_argument(\"--threshold\", type=int, default=DEFAULT_THRESHOLD)\n408\t    ap.add_argument(\"--include\", action=\"append\", default=None, metavar=\"GLOB\")\n409\t    ap.add_argument(\"--baseline\", default=BASELINE)\n410\t    args = ap.parse_args(argv)\n411\t\n412\t    root = Path(args.root).resolve()\n413\t    rows, modules = census(root, tuple(args.include or DEFAULT_INCLUDE))\n414\t    if args.report or args.mode == \"report\":\n415\t        _report(rows, modules, args.threshold)\n416\t        return 0\n417\t\n418\t    over = [r for r in rows if r[2] > args.threshold]\n419\t    path = root / args.baseline\n420\t    old = read_baseline(path)\n421\t    if args.restamp:\n422\t        write_baseline(path, over, old)\n423\t        print(\n424\t            \"check_complexity: re-stamped {} row(s) -> {}\".format(\n425\t                len(over), args.baseline\n426\t            )\n427\t        )\n428\t        return 0\n429\t\n430\t    grew, improved = compare(over, old)\n431\t    if not grew and not improved:\n432\t        print(\n433\t            \"check_complexity: OK - {} row(s) over {}, unchanged from baseline.\".format(\n434\t                len(over), args.threshold\n435\t            )\n436\t        )\n437\t        return 0\n438\t    _fail(grew, improved)\n439\t    return 1 if args.mode == \"enforce\" else 0\n440\t\n441\t\n442\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n443\t    sys.exit(main())\n444\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca6212b0-f544-4b45-8dd8-4a008302532c","uuid":"cd6a3f75-199c-4f1d-a1e2-3860cca34a66","timestamp":"2026-08-30T16:03:44.746Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template-drive\\wi-537-complexity-sensor-report-only\\project-trajectory\\scripts\\check_complexity.py","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"check_complexity.py — the stdlib cognitive-complexity + SLOC census.\n\nMeasures SonarSource Cognitive Complexity (CognitiveComplexity.pdf v1.7) and\nSLOC per function, plus a per-module public-symbol count (reported, never\ngated — the Ousterhout depth proxy). Stdlib only: a shipped check must not make\nevery adopter install a linter, which is why this exists beside — and not on\ntop of — the ruff C901 ratchet. Because the counting rules live in this\ndocstring rather than in a third-party tool, the baseline is a property of the\ncode, not of a tool version: a linter upgrade cannot silently invalidate it.\n\nREPORT-ONLY as shipped: no `[step:]` is wired into `docs/stack.ini`, so nothing\nruns this as a gate. The enforce capability exists (`--mode enforce`, exercised\nby the tests) but arming it is an opt-in act — a `[step:complexity]` step, of\nthe shape below:\n\n    [step:complexity]\n    command = {py} project-trajectory/scripts/check_complexity.py --root .\n\nCENSUS UNIT. One row per module-level function and per method. A nested `def`\nor `lambda` is scored INTO its enclosing function (Sonar charges the nesting\nincrement for it, never a base increment) and never gets its own row: the\nreward for decomposition is a SIBLING function or a new module, not an inner\none, and a second row for the inner def would double-count it in the census.\n\nBASELINE. `docs/complexity-baseline`, TSV, LF-only, sorted by path then\nfunction, one row per over-threshold function: path, function, cognitive, sloc,\nreason. Its own header states that it is a DEBT STATEMENT, not an approval. TSV\nfor one reason — the minimum merge-conflict surface when two concurrent\nsessions re-stamp; it matches the extension-less house data files under `docs/`\n(`docs/coverage-floors`). There is NO inline suppression pragma and there must\nnever be one; the escape hatch is this one reviewed central file, because a\nscattered opt-out self-replicates as new code copies it.\n\nImplements: SR-183, LLR-206\n\"\"\"\n\nimport argparse\nimp